u_A-u_B)\\right|.$$\n\nComputing the differences:\n$$u_B-u_C=-\\tfrac{32}{65},\\quad u_C-u_A=-\\tfrac{4}{5},\\quad u_A-u_B=\\tfrac{84}{65}.$$\n\n$$\\text{Sum}=3\\!\\left(-\\tfrac{32}{65}\\right)+\\tfrac{69}{65}\\!\\left(-\\tfrac{4}{5}\\right)+\\tfrac{29}{5}\\!\\left(\\tfrac{84}{65}\\right)=-\\tfrac{480}{325}-\\tfrac{276}{325}+\\tfrac{2436}{325}=\\tfrac{1680}{325}=\\tfrac{336}{65}.$$\n\n$$[A_3B_3C_3]=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\cdot\\tfrac{336}{65}=\\tfrac{168\\sqrt{14}}{65}.$$\n\n**Final ratio:**\n$$\\frac{[A_3B_3C_3]}{[ABC]}=\\frac{168\\sqrt{14}/65}{12\\sqrt{14}}=\\frac{168}{780}=\\frac{14}{65}.$$\n\nSince $\\gcd(14,65)=1$, we have $a=14$, $b=65$, and\n\n$$a+b=\\boxed{79}.$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this geometry problem. Triangle ABC with sides AB=9, BC=10, CA=13. Incircle touches AB at C1, BC at A1, CA at B1. A2 is reflection of A1 over midpoint of BC. Similarly B2, C2. A3 is intersection of AA2 with incircle closer to A. Similarly B3, C3. Find [A3B3C3]/[ABC].\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Let me place the triangle and compute everything.\n\nFirst, let me find the incircle. Sides: a=BC=10, b=CA=13, c=AB=9. Semiperimeter s=(10+13+9)/2=16. Area of ABC: using Heron, [ABC]=sqrt(16·6·3·7)=sqrt(2016)=sqrt(2016). 2016=16·126=16·9·14=144·14. So [ABC]=12√14.\n\nInradius r=[ABC]/s=12√14/16=3√14/4.\n\nContact points: A1 on BC, with BA1 = s-b = 16-13 = 3, CA1 = s-c = 16-9 = 7. So A1 divides BC with BA1=3, A1C=7.\nB1 on CA: CB1 = s-c = 7, B1A = s-a = 6. So CB1=7, B1A=6.\nC1 on AB: AC1 = s-a = 6, C1B = s-b = 3. So AC1=6, C1B=3.\n\nMidpoint of BC: M_a. A2 is reflection of A1 over M_a. Since A1 is at distance 3 from B along BC (length 10), and midpoint is at distance 5 from B. Reflection: A2 is at distance 5+(5-3)=7 from B, i.e., distance 3 from C. So A2 is on BC with BA2=7, A2C=3.\n\nSimilarly B2 on CA: B1 is at distance 7 from C (CB1=7), midpoint of CA is at distance 13/2=6.5 from C. Reflection: B2 at distance 6.5+(6.5-7)=6 from C. So CB2=6, B2A=7.\n\nC2 on AB: C1 at distance 6 from A (AC1=6), midpoint of AB at distance 9/2=4.5 from A. Reflection: C2 at distance 4.5+(4.5-6)=3 from A. So AC2=3, C2B=6.\n\nNow I need to find A3 = intersection of line AA2 with incircle, closer to A. Similarly for B3, C3.\n\nThis is a computational geometry problem. Let me set up coordinates.\n\nLet me place B at origin, C at (10,0). Then find A.\nAB=9, AC=13, BC=10.\nA = (x,y) with x²+y²=81, (x-10)²+y²=169.\nx²-20x+100+y²=169 → 81-20x+100=169 → -20x=-12 → x=3/5.\ny²=81-9/25=(2025-9)/25=2016/25. y=√2016/5=12√14/5.\n\nSo A=(3/5, 12√14/5), B=(0,0), C=(10,0).\n\nIncircle center I: I = (a·A + b·B + c·C)/(a+b+c) where a=BC=10, b=CA=13, c=AB=9.\nWait, the incenter formula: I = (a·A + b·B + c·C)/(a+b+c) where a is side opposite A (BC=10), b opposite B (CA=13), c opposite C (AB=9).\nI = (10·A + 13·B + 9·C)/32.\n10·A = (6, 120√14/5)... wait let me compute. 10·(3/5, 12√14/5) = (6, 24√14).\n13·B = (0,0).\n9·C = (90, 0).\nSum = (96, 24√14). I = (96/32, 24√14/32) = (3, 3√14/4).\n\nCheck: inradius r = 3√14/4, and distance from I to BC (y=0) is 3√14/4. ✓.\n\nNow A1 on BC: BA1=3, so A1=(3,0). A2 on BC with BA2=7: A2=(7,0).\n\nB1 on CA: CB1=7. C=(10,0), A=(3/5, 12√14/5). Direction from C to A: A-C = (3/5-10, 12√14/5) = (-47/5, 12√14/5). Length = 13. Unit vector = (-47/65, 12√14/65). B1 = C + 7·(unit) = (10 - 7·47/65, 7·12√14/65) = (10 - 329/65, 84√14/65) = ((650-329)/65, 84√14/65) = (321/65, 84√14/65).\n\nMidpoint of CA: ((10+3/5)/2, (0+12√14/5)/2) = (53/10, 6√14/5).\nB2 = reflection of B1 over midpoint = 2·midpoint - B1 = (106/10 - 321/65, 12√14/5 - 84√14/65).\n106/10 = 53/5. 53/5 - 321/65 = (689-321)/65 = 368/65.\n12√14/5 = 156√14/65. 156√14/65 - 84√14/65 = 72√14/65.\nB2 = (368/65, 72√14/65).\n\nCheck: CB2 should be 6. Distance from C(10,0) to B2(368/65, 72√14/65): (10-368/65)² + (72√14/65)² = ((650-368)/65)² + (72²·14/65²) = (282/65)² + (72576/4225) = (79524+72576)/4225 = 152100/4225 = 36. ✓ Distance=6.\n\nC1 on AB: AC1=6. A=(3/5,12√14/5), B=(0,0). Direction A to B: B-A = (-3/5, -12√14/5), length 9. Unit = (-1/15, -4√14/15). C1 = A + 6·unit = (3/5 - 6/15, 12√14/5 - 24√14/15) = (3/5 - 2/5, 36√14/15 - 24√14/15) = (1/5, 12√14/15) = (1/5, 4√14/5).\n\nMidpoint of AB: (3/10, 6√14/5).\nC2 = 2·mid - C1 = (3/5 - 1/5, 12√14/5 - 4√14/5) = (2/5, 8√14/5).\n\nCheck: AC2 should be 3. Distance from A(3/5,12√14/5) to C2(2/5,8√14/5): (1/5)² + (4√14/5)² = 1/25 + 224/25 = 225/25 = 9. ✓.\n\nNow I need line AA2 and its intersection with incircle.\n\nA = (3/5, 12√14/5), A2 = (7, 0).\nLine AA2: parametrize P = A + t(A2 - A) = (3/5 + t(7-3/5), 12√14/5 + t(0-12√14/5)) = (3/5 + t·32/5, 12√14/5·(1-t)).\nAt t=0: A. At t=1: A2.\n\nIncircle: (x-3)² + (y - 3√14/4)² = (3√14/4)² = 9·14/16 = 126/16 = 63/8.\n\nSubstitute:\nx = 3/5 + 32t/5, y = 12√14/5·(1-t).\n\nx - 3 = 3/5 + 32t/5 - 3 = 3/5 - 15/5 + 32t/5 = (-12 + 32t)/5 = (32t-12)/5.\ny - 3√14/4 = 12√14/5·(1-t) - 3√14/4 = √14·(12(1-t)/5 - 3/4) = √14·((48(1-t) - 15)/20) = √14·(48-48t-15)/20 = √14·(33-48t)/20.\n\nSo:\n((32t-12)/5)² + 14·((33-48t)/20)² = 63/8.\n\n(32t-12)²/25 + 14·(33-48t)²/400 = 63/8.\n\nMultiply by 400:\n16·(32t-12)² + 14·(33-48t)² = 400·63/8 = 50·63 = 3150.\n\n(32t-12)² = 1024t² - 768t + 144.\n16·(1024t²-768t+144) = 16384t² - 12288t + 2304.\n\n(33-48t)² = 2304t² - 3168t + 1089.\n14·(2304t²-3168t+1089) = 32256t² - 44352t + 15246.\n\nSum: 48640t² - 56640t + 17550 = 3150.\n48640t² - 56640t + 14400 = 0.\nDivide by 160: 304t² - 354t + 90 = 0.\nDivide by 2: 152t² - 177t + 45 = 0.\n\nDiscriminant: 177² - 4·152·45 = 31329 - 27360 = 3969 = 63².\nt = (177 ± 63)/(304).\nt1 = (177-63)/304 = 114/304 = 57/152.\nt2 = (177+63)/304 = 240/304 = 15/19.\n\nA3 is closer to A, so smaller t. t = 57/152.\n\nHmm wait, let me double check. A is at t=0, A2 at t=1. The incircle is between them. The line from A to A2 passes through the incircle. A is outside the incircle (vertex), A2 is on BC which is tangent to incircle at A1=(3,0)... wait A2=(7,0) is on BC, and the incircle touches BC at A1=(3,0). So A2 is 